---
import { SITE_TITLE, NAV_ITEMS } from '../consts';
---

<header class="header">
  <div class="wrap header__inner">
    {/* Brand lockup: an accent tile carrying your initial, plus the lowercase
        wordmark closed by an accent full stop. Presentation only — SITE_TITLE
        stays capitalised for <title> and Open Graph. Mark and dot are
        aria-hidden so the accessible name is just the site name.

        The initial is derived from SITE_TITLE rather than hardcoded, so it
        follows the name you set in consts.ts with nothing else to change.
        A flexbox tile rather than an inline SVG <text>: SVG text centring
        depends on `dominant-baseline`, which browsers still disagree about,
        and it cannot inherit the system font stack cleanly. */}
    <a class="header__brand" href="/">
      <span class="header__mark" aria-hidden="true"
        >{SITE_TITLE.charAt(0).toUpperCase()}</span
      >
      <span
        >{SITE_TITLE.toLowerCase()}<span
          class="header__brand-dot"
          aria-hidden="true">.</span
        ></span
      >
    </a>

    <nav aria-label="Primary">
      {/* `role="list"` is NOT redundant — keep it, and add it to any list you
          add. WebKit drops the implicit list role from a `<ul>` whose
          `list-style` is `none`, so VoiceOver stops announcing "list, N items";
          the role restores it. Every `<ul>` in this template removes its
          bullets, so every one carries this. */}
      <ul class="header__list" role="list">
        {
          NAV_ITEMS.map((item) => (
            <li>
              <a class="header__link" href={item.href}>
                {item.label}
              </a>
            </li>
          ))
        }
      </ul>
    </nav>
  </div>
</header>

{/* Global because it feeds `scroll-padding-top` on <html>, which a scoped rule
    cannot reach — and it belongs to this component, not to global.css, because
    the number is a property of this header. */}
<style is:global>
  /* Clearance for in-page anchors under the sticky header, consumed by
     `scroll-padding-top` in global.css. The header wraps as the viewport
     narrows and its height steps at every wrap point, so how many heights it
     has is a property of your nav — not a fixed two. With the placeholder name
     and the three labels this template ships it is two, measured across 228
     widths from 280px to 2560px:

       ≤ 373px   102.6–103.0px   brand and nav on separate rows
       ≥ 374px   65.3–71.4px     one row; the range is the fluid brand type,
                                 which stops growing at 1353px

     Each value below clears its case by at least 4.6px. Note the breakpoint is
     23.5rem (376px), not the 374px where the header actually stops wrapping —
     it is rounded UP from the measured transition, and up is the safe
     direction: too high only leaves extra space above the heading you jumped
     to, too low lets the header cover it outright. The cost is that 374–375px
     takes the taller offset against the shorter header and over-clears by
     ~43px, and 375px is a common phone width. Accepted: what a margin buys
     against is sub-pixel disagreement at the boundary, and on the low side that
     failure is the header covering the heading you jumped to.

     BOTH TRANSITIONS MOVED when the phone-width tightening at the bottom of
     this file was added, and for these three labels no height did. The
     single-row point came in from 415px to 374px and this breakpoint from 27rem
     to 23.5rem, while both heights above stayed within 0.4px of what they were.

     DO NOT READ THAT AS "TIGHTENING CANNOT MOVE A HEIGHT" — it can, and the
     worked example below is where it does. Only the header's own `column-gap`
     is height-neutral. `.header__list` is tightened with the `gap` SHORTHAND,
     which sets `row-gap` too, 24→12px — so every state where the nav's own
     list WRAPS gets 12px shorter per extra row. Measured on the worked example:
     its two wrapped states came down 184.2→172.2px and 152.2→140.2px, exactly
     12px each. The three labels shipped here never wrap THEIR OWN LIST anywhere
     in 280–2560px — the header wraps, per the table above, but the list itself
     stays on one line — so there is no such state to move. Yours may have one.

     Editing that media query moves these transitions again — re-measure the
     breakpoints AND the values, and re-measure a wrapped-list height if you
     have one.

     TODO: re-measure after setting SITE_TITLE and NAV_ITEMS, and watch THREE
     widths rather than one:

       1. where the nav drops below the brand — raise the 23.5rem breakpoint
          above it if it moved;
       2. where the nav's OWN list wraps onto a second line. That is a third,
          taller state and two values cannot express it. The bare `:root` rule
          below is the tallest case: raise it to clear the wrapped-list height,
          then add a `min-width` query between it and the 23.5rem one for the
          middle case, mirroring how the two below already step down;
       3. how far up the phone-width block at the bottom of this file needs to
          reach. Its 25.875rem cap is a measurement of THIS nav too: a wrap
          point above 414px is one the tightening cannot move, however much it
          may still help at narrower widths. Raise the cap past your own wrap
          point.

     Checking only (1) is the trap, and it is not hypothetical. Swapping in a
     longer name and a fourth label — "Alexandra Montgomery", with Work ·
     Services · About · Contact — takes the single-row point from the 374px
     above out to 599px, makes the nav's own list wrap at 285px (140.2px tall)
     and the wordmark wrap as well at 284px (172.2px). Left at the values below,
     that nav puts the header over the heading at 113 of 228 widths sampled
     between 280px and 2560px: by 27–29px through the whole 376–598px band, by
     32px where the list wraps and by 64px where the wordmark wraps too. Four
     states, from a change to two constants in consts.ts.

     599px is where that nav goes one row BOTH with the tightening and without
     it, and that is (3) stated as a measurement. Its single-row transition sits
     above the 25.875rem cap, so nothing at the bottom of this file, AS CAPPED,
     can move it. That is (3)'s instruction to raise the cap, not an argument
     against it. Read it narrowly the other way too: the block still applies to
     that nav BELOW the cap and does real work there — see the trade below — but
     the one width it most needs fixed is the one a capped tightening cannot
     reach.

     THE TIGHTENING WAS NOT A NET GAIN FOR THAT NAV, AND THE HONEST FORM OF THAT
     IS A TRADE RATHER THAN A VERDICT. Measured both ways over the same 228
     widths: 93 failing before, 113 after. It FIXED 36 widths — 286–321px, where
     the shorter INTER-ITEM gap (the column half of that same shorthand, not the
     row half above) is enough to keep the list on one line; below 286px it
     wraps anyway — and cut the worst over-clear from 76.2px to 64.2px. It BROKE
     56 (376–431px), because the breakpoint above came down with the transition
     it was measured against. A shorter nav gains 38px of viewport on a phone.
     All of it is measurements of the three labels shipped here, and none of it
     survives you editing consts.ts.

     A single clamp() cannot express any of this: the height steps down at each
     wrap point and clamp() is monotonic. */
  :root {
    --header-offset: 6.75rem;
  }

  @media (min-width: 23.5rem) {
    :root {
      --header-offset: 4.75rem;
    }
  }
</style>

<style>
  /* No hamburger menu: that would need JavaScript. Instead the nav wraps onto
     a second row on narrow screens, which stays usable and ships 0 KB. */
  .header {
    position: sticky;
    top: 0;
    z-index: 10;
    border-block-end: 1px solid var(--c-line);
    /* Solid rather than translucent — a blurred backdrop costs GPU work on
       scroll for a decorative effect. */
    background-color: var(--c-bg);
  }

  .header__inner {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: space-between;
    gap: var(--space-xs) var(--space-m);
    padding-block: var(--space-s);
  }

  .header__brand {
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 0.5em;
    font-weight: 700;
    font-size: var(--step-1);
    letter-spacing: -0.04em;
    color: var(--c-text);
    text-decoration: none;
  }

  /* Sized in em so the mark tracks the fluid brand type size. Inline-flex
     centring works with any letter, in any font, without the SVG text
     baseline guesswork. */
  .header__mark {
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: center;
    width: 1.5em;
    height: 1.5em;
    border-radius: 0.35em;
    background-color: var(--c-accent);
    color: var(--c-accent-contrast);
    /* The tile reads as a mark, not as running text, so it wants its own
       optical size rather than inheriting the brand's 1.1em. */
    font-size: 0.7em;
    font-weight: 700;
    letter-spacing: 0;
  }

  .header__brand-dot {
    color: var(--c-accent);
  }

  .header__list {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: var(--space-m);
    list-style: none;
    padding: 0;
  }

  .header__link {
    color: var(--c-muted);
    text-decoration: none;
    transition: color var(--transition);
  }

  .header__link:hover {
    color: var(--c-text);
  }

  /* Phone widths only: buy back enough inline space that the brand and the nav
     share one row instead of the nav dropping beneath it. Two levers, both of
     them whitespace — the header's own column gap (24→8px) and the list's
     inter-item gap (24→12px). With the placeholder name and these three labels
     that moves the single-row point from 415px to 374px, so a 375px or 390px
     phone gets a 65px sticky header instead of a 103px one. Measured, not
     estimated: at 390px the brand is 129px and the list 213px, which with the
     24px gap needs 366px against the 342px a 390px viewport leaves inside
     `.wrap` — 24px short, and the two gaps return 40px.

     A THIRD LEVER EXISTS AND IS DELIBERATELY NOT TAKEN. `.header__link` sets no
     `font-size`, so every label inherits body's --step-0 (~16.1px at 390px)
     where --step--1 would be ~13.4px — about 17% of every label's width sitting
     untried. Adding `font-size: var(--step--1)` to the block below takes the
     single-row point to 348px, measured, which is what 360px-class Androids
     need. It is left out because it is the one lever that costs something when
     it is NOT needed: the two gaps are harmless on a nav that already fits,
     while a type size shrinks labels this template cannot see, for an adopter
     who may have two short ones and no wrapping to fix. If yours still wraps at
     a width you care about, that declaration is the next thing to add — and
     then re-measure the block above, because it moves both transitions.

     Capped at 25.875rem (414px) because at 415px the untightened header is
     already one row and has nothing to fix; tightening desktop nav spacing
     would be a cost bought for nothing. The cap sits just below that measured
     transition, so no band is left both untightened and wrapping.

     These are measurements, not universal figures — re-measure after setting
     SITE_TITLE and NAV_ITEMS. Method, which needs no tooling beyond a browser:
     build, serve `dist/`, and from a page on that same origin load `/index.html`
     into an `<iframe>` you resize across 280–2560px, setting the frame
     document's `documentElement.style.overflow = 'hidden'` so its own scrollbar
     does not steal layout width. At each width record `.header`'s height and
     the resolved `scroll-padding-top` on `<html>`, and assert the second is
     never less than the first.

     ONE INSTRUMENT NEEDS CARE. Test brand-and-nav-on-one-row by whether their
     vertical ranges OVERLAP, not by counting distinct `top` values:
     `.header__inner` is `align-items: center`, so two children of different
     heights sharing a row have different tops, and a distinct-tops count reads
     "wrapped" at every width including 2560px. That is a check that cannot
     fail. Counting distinct tops among `.header__list li` is fine and is how
     you find the nav's own wrap — those are siblings of equal height. */
  @media (max-width: 25.875rem) {
    .header__inner {
      column-gap: var(--space-2xs);
    }

    .header__list {
      gap: var(--space-xs);
    }
  }
</style>
